      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a20.8.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g20.7.17', 'g20.8.15', 'g20.8.17'],
          lemma: "εἶδεν καὶ ἐπίστευσεν",
          title: "The grave clothes, and the first faith",
          body: "The detail John lingers over is the *arrangement* of the burial cloths — and John was there, an eyewitness recording what his own eyes took in. The linen strips lay collapsed where the body had been; the face-cloth was **folded up in a place by itself.** This is not the scene of a robbery. Grave-robbers do not unwrap a corpse and leave the valuable spice-soaked linens behind, neatly ordered; and no thief pauses to fold the napkin. The cloths lay like a chrysalis a butterfly has left — undisturbed, as though the body had simply *passed through* them and gone. Seeing this, the beloved disciple **“saw and believed.”** He is the first human being to believe in the resurrection, and he believes not because he has yet seen the risen Lord — he has not — but because he reads the silent evidence of an empty grave and orderly cloths and *understands.* John is careful to add (v. 9) that they did not yet grasp from Scripture that he must rise; the faith came first, sparked by the sign, and understanding followed. It is a small parable of how faith often works: a heart open to Jesus sees the same evidence a skeptic dismisses, and *believes.*",
        },
      ],
