      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a20.16.1',
          kind: "reading",
          refs: ['g20.16.4', 'g20.16.10', 'g20.15.13'],
          lemma: undefined,
          title: "“Mary.” — “Rabboni!”",
          body: "It is one of the most intimate moments in all of Scripture, and one of the most quietly devastating in its beauty. Mary has stood weeping at the tomb, has seen angels and not been comforted, has spoken to the risen Lord himself and mistaken him for the gardener. Grief has blinded her; she is looking for a corpse and cannot see the living Christ standing before her. And then he speaks one word — **her name.** *“Mary.”* And instantly she knows. What all the evidence could not do, what the angels could not do, what even the sight of his face could not do, the sound of her own name on his lips accomplishes in a heartbeat: she turns and cries, **“Rabboni!”** — *my Teacher, my Master.* This is the Good Shepherd who said, *“he calls his own sheep by name… and they know his voice”* (10:3–4). He does not appear to Mary as a stranger to be argued into recognition; he simply calls her, personally, and the knowing is immediate and total. Here is the heart of the resurrection faith: the risen Lord is not a doctrine to be deduced but a person who knows us by name and calls us out of our grief. Every Christian’s story is, in miniature, this scene — Jesus speaking a name, and a weeping heart turning to find that the One it thought was lost is alive, and *present*, and knows exactly who we are.",
        },
      ],
