      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a20.22.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g20.21.15', 'g20.22.4', 'g20.22.9'],
          lemma: "καθὼς ἀπέσταλκέν με … Λάβετε Πνεῦμα Ἅγιον",
          title: "Peace, sending, and the breath of new life",
          body: "The risen Lord does three things for the frightened men behind locked doors, and together they remake the church. First, **peace** — twice: *“Peace be with you.”* The first who might have come with a rebuke (they had all abandoned him) comes with a benediction; and he shows them the wounds, so they will know that the peace is bought, that the hands and side that prove he is risen also prove *how* he saved them. Second, **commission:** *“As the Father sent me, even so I am sending you.”* The mission of Jesus does not end with Jesus; it flows on into his people, who are sent into the world with the same purpose and on the same terms — to embody and proclaim the love of God, and, like him, to be sent *into* the world, not out of it. Third, **the breath of the Spirit:** he *breathed on them* and said, *“Receive the Holy Spirit.”* John surely means us to hear Genesis 2:7, where God breathed into the man of dust and he became a living soul. This is new creation. As the first Adam was made alive by God’s breath, the new humanity is made alive by the breath of the risen Christ — a real anticipation of Pentecost, when the Spirit will come in fullness. The pattern is complete: forgiven and given peace, sent as the Father sent the Son, and empowered by the very breath of God. A trembling group of failures is turned, in one evening, into the apostolic church.",
        },
      ],
